-- title:
--   Theorem 4.17: Existence of Euler affine pairs
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g$ be admissible in its structural presentation, with $\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g)\subseteq[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]$, and let $x=x_z+x_V+x_l$ be ad-nilpotent with pointed $C_x$. An Euler derivation $D$ satisfying $Dx=x$ and $D(\mathfrak l)\subseteq\mathfrak l$ exists if and only if there is an Euler element $h\in\mathfrak l$ and a splitting $V_h=A\oplus B$ into $\mathfrak l$-submodules such that
--
--   $$[h,x_l]=x_l,\quad x_z\in[A,A],\quad x_V\in A,$$
--
--   and the sum of $[A,A]$, $[B,B]$, and $[A,B]+[V_{h,0},V_{h,0}]$ is direct. Here $V_h=[h,V]$ and $V_{h,0}=\ker\rho(h)$. The zero Euler derivation is allowed. No image condition on $x_V$ is assumed.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 25, Theorem 4.17: Existence of Euler affine pairs

